priya.nair@devfleet.io|Re: Quick question | byLLM structured output|Hi Priya,

Great question — and good news: byLLM does support typed/structured outputs natively, so you won't need to manually parse the model's plain-text response.

In byLLM, you can declare a function with a typed return value and delegate its implementation to an LLM using the `by llm()` syntax. The runtime enforces that the model's output conforms to the declared return type, so structured fields come back ready to use — no post-processing required.

For your log triage use case, you could define a return type with the three fields you mentioned (severity, component, message), and the model will populate those fields directly from the input log line. Something like:

```
record LogEntry {{
    severity: str;
    component: str;
    message: str;
}}

fn triage_log(line: str) -> LogEntry by llm() {}
```

When you call `triage_log("ERROR | walker_spawn | context missing")`, byLLM will return a structured `LogEntry` object with the fields populated — no string parsing on your end.

This should fit your pipeline cleanly. Let me know if you'd like a more detailed walkthrough or if you run into anything during your evaluation — happy to help.

Best,
Impact Team

